# No SMTP settings and no Telegram bot token here. The contact form posts to the C# backend,
# which sends the mail through its one configured relay and posts the enquiry to Telegram
# itself; the recipient is Notifications__ContactEmail, the relay credentials are the API's
# SMTP_* and the bot is TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN / TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID, all in the repository root's
# .env. Nothing in client/ reads any of them, and adding one back puts a credential in a
# browser-facing container that has no other reason to hold one.

# WHMCS address, for the client-area links that hand a visitor off to a WHMCS install
# (card management, invoice payment, domain transfer). An address only, and deliberately
# no API credential: the WHMCS API is called by hostpanel-api, never by this app, so the
# identifier/secret pair that used to sit here had no reader and only gave a
# browser-facing container a secret to leak. Left empty, every link that needs it is
# hidden rather than broken.
#
# This file is for `yarn dev` run directly in client/, where nuxt.config.ts reads
# process.env at startup and this name works. A containerised deployment builds the app
# first, which freezes the default, so the compose overlays pass the repository root's
# WHMCS_URL under Nitro's override name NUXT_PUBLIC_WHMCS_URL instead. Same value, same
# single variable in .env -- only the spelling the container sees differs.
WHMCS_URL=https://your-whmcs-domain.com
